\subsection{Audio \& Mechanical Testing}

Mechanical and audio subsystems underwent functional testing to verify reliability and performance under repeated use conditions. Mechanical durability testing focused on the enclosure structure, hinged lid mechanism, and magnetic connector interfaces between blocks. Repeated assembly and disassembly cycles were performed to simulate typical classroom usage. Throughout testing, the magnetic connectors maintained reliable alignment and electrical contact through the pogo-pin interfaces, and the enclosure structure showed no signs of deformation or mechanical fatigue.

Thermal performance of the enclosure was also evaluated during extended operation. The passive ventilation holes integrated into the enclosure provided adequate airflow, allowing heat generated by the internal electronics, including the ESP32 microcontroller, display, and audio amplifier, to dissipate without requiring active cooling. Internal temperatures remained within safe operating limits during continuous system operation.

Audio subsystem testing was conducted to evaluate amplifier performance and sound clarity across the expected output range. The LM386 audio amplifier successfully drove the integrated 8-$\Omega$ speaker while maintaining stable output and minimal distortion. Audio playback of musical tones and sound effects remained clear without clipping at maximum volume levels. The bass boost configuration improved the perceived fullness of the audio output, which is particularly beneficial for small enclosure speakers.

Overall, testing confirmed that both the mechanical and audio subsystems meet the durability and functional requirements necessary for an interactive educational device designed for repeated handling and classroom use.

\section{Results and Discussion}
Blocks o' Code v3 successfully operates as a cohesive unit. Users can seamlessly attach blocks, view their generated logic on the app, and execute programs with immediate physical feedback. Future iterations will focus on further optimizing the mechanical connector tolerances and expanding the library of available block types.

\section{Conclusion}

Blocks o' Code v3 demonstrates the successful implementation of a modular embedded system tailored for interactive programming education. By integrating ESP32 control, I\textsuperscript{2}C communication, tactile hardware, and a responsive Flutter application, the project transforms abstract coding concepts into an intuitive, hands-on experience for beginners.

The system combines multiple hardware subsystems, including touchscreen displays, LED matrices, programmable lighting, audio feedback, and magnetic pogo-pin connectivity, to create a flexible and engaging platform for learning computational thinking. The modular cube architecture allows users to physically construct program sequences while receiving immediate visual and auditory feedback, reinforcing the relationship between program structure and system behavior. The standardized hardware design also ensures compatibility between blocks while simplifying manufacturing and system integration.

Testing results demonstrate that the mechanical, electrical, and communication subsystems operate reliably under repeated use conditions. The enclosure design provides durability and adequate thermal management, while the communication architecture enables stable data transfer between blocks and the central controller. Together, these components form a cohesive system capable of supporting interactive educational applications.

Overall, Blocks o' Code v3 provides a scalable and engaging platform for introducing programming concepts through physical interaction. By combining modular embedded hardware with intuitive software interfaces, the system offers an accessible approach to programming education that encourages experimentation, creativity, and hands-on learning.

